/*!
    \page style-reference-menus-views.html
    \title Styling Menus and Item Views
    \brief The style elements, states, and options of menus, menu bars, item
    view headers, tree branch indicators, and item view items.

    This page is part of the \l{Widget Style Reference}. For an explanation
    of the element trees, screenshots, and tables, see that page. The common
    state flags and members that every widget sets are listed in
    \l{How a Style Draws a Widget#Common state flags and members}{How a Style
    Draws a Widget}.

    \section1 Menus

    \l QMenu keeps a list of actions and draws each as a menu item. On a paint
    event, it draws the panel of the menu, then each item with \c CE_MenuItem
    and a \l QStyleOptionMenuItem, and the frame last. Menu items have no
    separate label element, so \c CE_MenuItem draws the check mark, icon, text,
    shortcut, and submenu arrow.

    \list
    \li \c PE_PanelMenu, the background
    \li \c CE_MenuItem, once per action (\c PM_SmallIconSize)
        \list
        \li \c PE_IndicatorMenuCheckMark, for a checked item. Many styles use
            \c PE_IndicatorCheckBox and \c PE_IndicatorRadioButton instead.
        \li \c PE_IndicatorArrowRight or \c PE_IndicatorArrowLeft, for an item
            that opens a submenu
        \endlist
    \li \c CE_MenuScroller (\c PM_MenuScrollerHeight), when the menu is too tall
        for the screen and \c SH_Menu_Scrollable is set
    \li \c CE_MenuTearoff (\c PM_MenuTearoffHeight), for a
        \l{QMenu::tearOffEnabled}{tear-off} menu
    \li \c PE_FrameMenu (\c PM_MenuPanelWidth), the frame, with a
        \l QStyleOptionFrame whose \c lineWidth is \c PM_MenuPanelWidth
    \li \c CE_MenuEmptyArea, for the space not covered by items or frame
    \endlist

    \l QMenu lays out the items itself, using \c PM_MenuHMargin,
    \c PM_MenuVMargin, \c PM_MenuPanelWidth, and \c PM_MenuDesktopFrameWidth. It
    also consults many \c SH_Menu_ hints, such as \c SH_Menu_SubMenuPopupDelay,
    \c SH_Menu_MouseTracking, and \c SH_Menu_Mask.

    \image styles/menu.webp
           {Menu with an icon item, a checked item, a submenu item, a disabled
           item, and a plain item, with each item outlined}

    \l QMenu resets the state after calling \l{QStyleOption::}{initFrom()}, so
    the items carry only \c State_Enabled, \c State_Active, and the flags in
    this table:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Selected
            \li The item is highlighted and isn't a separator.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The item is pressed.
        \row
            \li \c State_DownArrow
            \li The item is a scroller that scrolls the menu down.
        \row
            \li \c State_Enabled
            \li The action is enabled. Disabled actions clear this flag even
                when the menu is enabled.
    \endtable

    The members of \l QStyleOptionMenuItem are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li menuItemType
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionMenuItem::}{MenuItemType} value: a normal item,
                the default item, a separator, a submenu, a scroller, a
                tear-off, a margin, or an empty area.
        \row
            \li checkType
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionMenuItem::}{CheckType} value: not checkable,
                exclusive, or non-exclusive.
        \row
            \li checked
            \li Whether the item is checked.
        \row
            \li menuHasCheckableItems
            \li Whether at least one item in the menu is checkable, so that the
                style can reserve space for check marks.
        \row
            \li menuRect
            \li The rectangle of the whole menu.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The item text. A tab character separates the text from the
                shortcut.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The item icon.
        \row
            \li maxIconWidth
            \li The width of the widest icon in the menu.
        \row
            \li reservedShortcutWidth
            \li The width reserved for the shortcut column.
        \row
            \li font
            \li The font of the item text.
    \endtable

    For \c CE_MenuScroller and \c CE_MenuTearoff, the menu sets only
    \c menuItemType, \c checkType, \c maxIconWidth, and
    \c reservedShortcutWidth. \c CE_MenuEmptyArea also sets \c menuRect.

    \section1 Menu bars

    \l QMenuBar draws each menu title with \c CE_MenuBarItem and a
    \l QStyleOptionMenuItemV2, the space after the last title with
    \c CE_MenuBarEmptyArea, and its panel with \c PE_PanelMenuBar and a
    \l QStyleOptionFrame. The drop-down menus are \l{QMenu}s; see \l{Menus}.

    \list
    \li \c CE_MenuBarItem, once per menu (\c PM_SmallIconSize)
    \li \c PE_PanelMenuBar (\c PM_MenuBarPanelWidth)
    \li \c CE_MenuBarEmptyArea
    \endlist

    The menu bar lays out the items with \c PM_MenuBarItemSpacing,
    \c PM_MenuBarHMargin, and \c PM_MenuBarVMargin, and sizes them with
    \c CT_MenuBarItem. The painter that \l QMenuBar passes for the panel is
    clipped to the four border strips, each \c PM_MenuBarPanelWidth wide, so the
    panel can only paint the border. The painter for the empty area is clipped
    to what the items and the border leave over. The bar consults
    \c SH_MenuBar_MouseTracking, \c SH_MenuBar_AltKeyNavigation, and
    \c SH_DrawMenuBarSeparator.

    On \macos, \l QMenuBar uses the native menu bar by default and doesn't draw
    anything. Call \l{QMenuBar::setNativeMenuBar()} to make it use the style.

    \image styles/menubar.webp
           {Menu bar with the menus File, Edit, View, and Help, with each item
           and the empty area outlined}

    \l QMenuBar fills in the option itself rather than calling
    \l{QStyleOption::}{initFrom()}. It sets \c State_Enabled when the bar is
    enabled, and these flags on the items:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Selected
            \li The item is highlighted.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The item's menu is open.
        \row
            \li \c State_HasFocus
            \li The menu bar has focus or has a current item.
        \row
            \li \c State_MouseOver
            \li The mouse is over the item.
    \endtable

    The menu bar uses these members of \l QStyleOptionMenuItem:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li menuRect
            \li The rectangle of the whole menu bar.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The title of the menu.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The icon of the menu. Few styles draw it.
    \endtable

    For the panel, \l QStyleOptionFrame::lineWidth is set to
    \c PM_MenuBarPanelWidth and \l{QStyleOptionFrame::}{midLineWidth} to 0.

    \section1 Item view headers

    \l QHeaderView draws each header section with \c CE_Header and a
    \l QStyleOptionHeaderV2. \l QCommonStyle splits it into the section
    background, the label, and the sort indicator:

    \list
    \li \c CE_Header, once per section (\c PM_HeaderMargin)
        \list
        \li \c CE_HeaderSection
        \li \c CE_HeaderLabel (\c SE_HeaderLabel)
        \li \c PE_IndicatorHeaderArrow (\c SE_HeaderArrow,
            \c PM_HeaderMarkSize), for the sorted section
        \endlist
    \li \c CE_HeaderEmptyArea, for the space after the last section
    \endlist

    The header view sizes its sections with \c CT_HeaderSection and uses
    \c PM_HeaderGripMargin for the area in which the user can drag a section
    boundary. \l QTableView draws the button in the upper-left corner, where the
    two headers meet, as a \c CE_Header. The hint \c SH_Header_ArrowAlignment
    places the sort indicator.

    \image styles/header.webp
           {Table with the header sections Name, Size, and Modified, with the
           sections, labels, and the sort indicator outlined}

    The header sets these state flags on a section:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Raised
            \li Always. The header sets it for every section.
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li This is the horizontal header above the view.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The section is pressed, or the section is selected and
                \l{QHeaderView::highlightSections}{highlightSections} is on.
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li An item in the section is selected and
                \l{QHeaderView::highlightSections}{highlightSections} is on.
        \row
            \li \c State_MouseOver
            \li The mouse is over the section. Set only when
                \l{QHeaderView::sectionsClickable}{sectionsClickable} is on.
    \endtable

    The members of \l QStyleOptionHeader are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li section
            \li The logical index of the section.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The section text.
        \row
            \li textAlignment
            \li The alignment of the text in the section.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The section icon.
        \row
            \li iconAlignment
            \li The alignment of the icon in the section.
        \row
            \li position
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionHeader::}{SectionPosition} value: the section's
                position relative to the other sections.
        \row
            \li selectedPosition
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionHeader::}{SelectedPosition} value: the position
                of the selected section relative to this one.
        \row
            \li sortIndicator
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionHeader::}{SortIndicator} value: no indicator,
                or the direction of the sort arrow.
        \row
            \li orientation
            \li Whether this is the horizontal header above the view or the
                vertical header beside it.
    \endtable

    \l QStyleOptionHeaderV2 adds \c textElideMode, the elide mode for text that
    doesn't fit, and \c isSectionDragTarget, which is set on the section that a
    dragged section is about to be dropped on.

    \section1 Tree branch indicators

    \l QTreeView draws the branch indicators, the lines and arrows that show the
    relationship between the nodes, with \c PE_IndicatorBranch. It draws one
    indicator per indentation level in front of each item, so a deeply nested
    item has several. The tree also draws the background of each row with
    \c PE_PanelItemViewRow and indents the levels by \c PM_TreeViewIndentation.

    Both elements take a \l QStyleOptionViewItem, so a style can also read the
    row's palette, font, and alternate-row feature flag. The kind of branch is
    encoded in the state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Item
            \li The indicator belongs to the item in this row, rather than to an
                ancestor. Draw the horizontal connector here.
        \row
            \li \c State_Children
            \li The item has children, so the indicator shows an expand or
                collapse arrow.
        \row
            \li \c State_Open
            \li The item is expanded.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sibling
            \li The node at this level has a sibling below, so a vertical line
                continues downward.
    \endtable

    An indicator with none of these flags is drawn for a level whose node has no
    sibling below; the style draws nothing there.

    \image styles/branchindicator.webp
           {Tree view with expanded and collapsed folders, with the branch
           indicator cells outlined and labeled by their state flags}

    \section1 Item view items

    Delegates paint the items in item views. Qt's default delegate,
    \l QStyledItemDelegate, draws \c CE_ItemViewItem with a
    \l QStyleOptionViewItem and calculates the item size with
    \c CT_ItemViewItem, so a style controls how items look without an
    accompanying delegate. \l QCommonStyle splits the element into the
    background, the check indicator, the decoration, the text, and the focus
    frame:

    \list
    \li \c CE_ItemViewItem
        \list
        \li \c PE_PanelItemViewItem
        \li \c PE_IndicatorItemViewItemCheck (\c SE_ItemViewItemCheckIndicator)
        \li The decoration, drawn with \l{QIcon::}{paint()}
            (\c SE_ItemViewItemDecoration)
        \li The text, laid out and drawn by \l QCommonStyle with eliding and
            wrapping (\c SE_ItemViewItemText)
        \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect (\c SE_ItemViewItemFocusRect)
        \endlist
    \endlist

    The views consult item view hints such as
    \c SH_ItemView_ShowDecorationSelected,
    \c SH_ItemView_ActivateItemOnSingleClick, and
    \c SH_ItemView_ArrowKeysNavigateIntoChildren. To support data types or roles
    that \l QStyledItemDelegate doesn't handle, write a custom delegate; see
    \l{Model/View Programming}.

    The state flags and members of \l QStyleOptionViewItem are described in its
    class documentation; the most important are \c State_Selected,
    \c State_HasFocus, \c checkState, \c decorationPosition,
    \c displayAlignment, \c features, \c icon, \c text, and \c viewItemPosition.

    \sa {Widget Style Reference}, {Styling Buttons and Input Widgets},
        {Styling Containers and Windows}, QStyledItemDelegate
*/
